# Routing{% if template == "rest-api" %} & Blueprints{% endif %}

{% if template == "rest-api" %}
## Blueprint structure

Every resource gets its own file under `src/{{ package_name }}/routes/`,
exporting a single `bp = Blueprint("items", __name__,
url_prefix="/items")` (swap `items`/`"/items"` for the resource name).
`create_app()` registers it once:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.routes.items import bp as items_bp
app.register_blueprint(items_bp)
```

Route handlers are plain functions decorated with `@bp.get(...)`,
`@bp.post(...)`, `@bp.delete(...)`, etc. — mirror `routes/items.py` for
a new resource rather than inventing a new shape. Don't register routes
directly on `app` outside `main.py`'s own `/` health-check route; every
resource, even a one-endpoint one, gets its own Blueprint file.

## Handlers are sync — no `await`

Flask here is WSGI. Every handler is `def`, never `async def`; there's
no event loop to hand control back to. See `references/gotchas.md` for
what this means for anything slow (a query, an outbound HTTP call).

## Request validation — manual, because Flask doesn't do it for you

Unlike FastAPI, Flask has no automatic request-body-to-model wiring.
Every handler that accepts a body validates it explicitly:

```python
from pydantic import ValidationError
from {{ package_name }}.schemas import ItemCreate, ItemRead

@bp.post("/")
def create_item():
    try:
        payload = ItemCreate.model_validate(request.get_json(force=True, silent=True) or {})
    except ValidationError as exc:
        return jsonify({"detail": exc.errors()}), 422
    ...
```

`request.get_json(force=True, silent=True) or {}` is deliberate:
`force=True` parses the body as JSON even if the client didn't send a
`Content-Type: application/json` header, `silent=True` makes a
malformed body return `None` instead of raising, and `or {}` turns that
`None` into an empty dict — so a bad/missing body becomes a normal
Pydantic `ValidationError` (→ a `422` with field-level detail) instead
of Flask's generic `400 Bad Request` for unparseable JSON. Copy this
exact shape for any new endpoint that accepts a body; don't call
`request.json` directly (raises on a bad body with a much less useful
error) or skip validation and pass `request.get_json()` straight to
your{% if database != "none" %} model/{% endif %} database layer.

## Response shape

Return `jsonify(model.model_dump())` for a single resource,
`jsonify([m.model_dump() for m in items])` for a list — never a bare
Pydantic model or ORM instance (`jsonify` doesn't know how to serialize
either). For non-`200` responses, return a `(body, status_code)` tuple:
`return jsonify(created.model_dump()), 201`, `return "", 204`,
`return jsonify({"detail": "Item not found"}), 404`. Mirror
`routes/items.py`'s status codes for new resources: `201` on create,
`204` on delete (empty body), `404` via the tuple form when not found,
`422` for a validation failure.

{% if database != "none" %}
## Reading/writing the database inside a handler

{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}
Use `db.session` (imported from `core.db`) — Flask-SQLAlchemy's scoped
session, already bound to the current app/request context:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.core.db import db
from {{ package_name }}.models import Item

items = db.session.execute(db.select(Item)).scalars().all()
```

Don't build a new engine or session anywhere else — `db` is the one
instance, initialized once in `create_app()` via `db.init_app(app)`.
{% else %}
Call `get_session()` (imported from `core.db`) — it returns a session
bound to the current app context via a `scoped_session`, torn down
automatically at the end of the request (see
`core/db.py`'s `app.teardown_appcontext(...)`):

```python
from sqlalchemy import select
from {{ package_name }}.core.db import get_session
from {{ package_name }}.models import Item

session = get_session()
items = session.execute(select(Item)).scalars().all()
```

Don't create engines/sessions ad hoc in a route — `get_session()` is
the one entry point.
{% endif %}
