{% if worker == "celery" %}
## Enqueuing background work from a handler

A handler stays fast by handing slow work to the Celery worker instead
of doing it inline — `main.py`'s `/tasks/add` route is the template:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.tasks.example import add

@app.post("/tasks/add")
def enqueue_add():
    task = add.delay(a, b)
    return jsonify({"task_id": task.id})
```

`.delay(...)` returns immediately with a task id; the actual work runs
in the separate `celery worker` process (see `worker.py`), not inline
in this request. Never call a `@celery_app.task`-decorated function
directly (`add(a, b)`) from a route expecting it to run in the
background — that just runs it synchronously, in-process, defeating the
point.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
## Route structure

Routes are decorated directly on the module-level `app` in `main.py`
(`@app.get("/")`), since this template has only the one route. If you
add more than a couple of routes, or anything that needs its own file,
switch to the `rest-api` template's shape instead of growing `main.py`
indefinitely: one `Blueprint` per resource under a new `routes/`
package, registered via `app.register_blueprint(...)` — generate a
throwaway `rest-api` project (or read its `routes/items.py`) for the
concrete shape.

## Handlers are sync — no `await`

Flask here is WSGI, not ASGI. Handlers are plain `def`; there is no
`await` and no benefit to writing `async def` — see
`references/gotchas.md`.

## Response shape

This template's one route returns a plain `dict`
(`{"message": "Hello, World!"}`) — Flask auto-serializes a `dict`
returned from a `2xx` route to JSON. That shortcut is fine for a route
this simple; once you're validating input or returning anything with a
real shape (an id, nested fields), reach for explicit Pydantic
models + `jsonify(...)`, the way the `rest-api` template's
`schemas.py`/routes do, rather than passing dicts around by hand.
